# Known, accepted drift. Format:
#   <slug>|<substring of the drift message>   # why, and what the fix is
#
# Narrower than .lint-skip, which exempts a tool from every check. An entry
# here keeps the tool checked for everything else, so --strict can gate on new
# drift while this backlog is worked through.
#
# A waiver is a promise to come back, not a fix. Deleting a line should be the
# last step of fixing the thing it describes.

# (No waivers. The last one — table-graphic's stale clubs-data.js mirror —
# closed 16/08/2026, and its closing is a lesson worth the lines: the waiver
# said fixing it "changes what the tool draws", but by then NOTHING in the
# tool read the mirror's colours — only its name list and crest path. The
# scary half of a waiver can quietly stop being true while the waiver keeps
# saying it. Re-verify a waiver's premise before scheduling the careful fix
# it asks for.)
